================================================================================
TEST 38393 — FDB table is synced across the stack
================================================================================
Date        : 2026-08-19
Bench       : tb470
DUT         : AT-IE520-28GSX two-member VCStack, virtual-chassis-id 3439,
              VMAC 0000.cd37.0d6f
              member 1 = S/N 264A23066, MAC 84e3.2787.0740, console /dev/u5
              member 2 = S/N 264A23052, MAC 84e3.2787.09c0, console /dev/u4
Software    : IE520-tb470.rel  (IE520-tomahawk_ie520-continuous.rel,
              /tftpboot build dated 2026-08-19, md5 6e7557cddae9a60c0475d2124429437f,
              sha256 09b2a5a3b4e1663048606f1105f65afce62a321942823f664f6ce65533e8cf58)
Bootloader  : 9.1.0 (U-Boot 2025.01-04844-gd2292467da4d, Jul 22 2026)

VERDICT: ALL 7 STEPS PASS.
         FDB is synced across both stack members, before and after a master
         reboot, in BOTH the software MAC table and the hardware (ASIC) table.
         Worst-case traffic outage across the master reboot was 814 ms against
         a 6-second expectation.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
TOPOLOGY  (test case asks for: Ixia -- Sw1 == DUT == Sw2 -- Ixia)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
No Ixia on this bench; tb470 generates and receives with tcpreplay/tcpdump.

  tb470 eth1 ── AR4050 p1.0.1 │ po1 ┬ p1.0.3 ── DUT port1.0.1 (member 1) ┐
                                    └ p1.0.4 ── DUT port2.0.9 (member 2) ┘ DUT po1
                                                                           vlan 393
  tb470 eth2 ── x230   p1.0.1 │ po1 ┬ p1.0.2 ── DUT port2.0.1 (member 2) ┐
                                    └ p1.0.4 ── DUT port1.0.9 (member 1) ┘ DUT po2

  SW1 = AR4050S-5G  (S/N A10401G214000005, /dev/u1, 115200)
  SW2 = x230-10GP   (S/N G26ZE80EN,        /dev/u0, 9600)


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STEP 1 — Configure the DUT into the topology                            PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Pre-existing config was left over from test 38394 (vlan 3001 "arp-test-m1" on
port1.0.1, vlan 3002 "arp-test-m2" on port2.0.1). Replaced with a dedicated
test vlan.

DUT:
  no lacp global-passive-mode enable        <- see NOTE 1
  vlan database / vlan 393 name fdb-test-38393 / vlan 393 state enable
  interface port1.0.1,port1.0.9,port2.0.1,port2.0.9
    switchport mode access ; switchport access vlan 393
  interface port1.0.1,port2.0.9  -> channel-group 1 mode active     (po1, to AR4050)
  interface port2.0.1,port1.0.9  -> channel-group 2 mode active     (po2, to x230)
  interface po1,po2 -> switchport mode access ; switchport access vlan 393
  interface vlan393 -> ip address 10.38.215.34/27
  vlan database / no vlan 3001 / no vlan 3002
  write

AR4050:  interface port1.0.3-1.0.4 -> channel-group 1 mode active ; po1 access vlan 1
x230:    interface port1.0.2,port1.0.4 -> channel-group 1 mode active ; po1 access vlan 100

Both LAGs verified formed and spanning the stack:
  Aggregator po1 : port1.0.1 synchronized , port2.0.9 synchronized
  Aggregator po2 : port1.0.9 synchronized , port2.0.1 synchronized
  AR4050 po1     : port1.0.3 synchronized , port1.0.4 synchronized
  x230   po1     : port1.0.2 synchronized , port1.0.4 synchronized
All four DUT ports "connected", vlan 393, a-full/a-1000.

NOTE 1 — `lacp global-passive-mode enable` was set on the DUT (and both
neighbours). With it on, the DUT silently auto-created its own single-member
aggregators the moment the neighbours started LACP:
    port1.0.1: Dynamically learned channel-group config is now in running-config
and then REFUSED further edits to those ports:
    % port1.0.1: Cannot configure aggregator member.
    % port2.0.9: Port port2.0.9 cannot be aggregated due to mismatching link configuration.
Deterministic control required turning global passive mode OFF, clearing the
dynamic groups, normalising all four ports to identical config, and only then
applying the channel-groups. Worth knowing before building any LAG on a box
that has this enabled.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STEP 2 — Configure setup and ports to forward traffic, 3000 MAC addresses  PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Two pcaps built with scapy (/tmp/mkfdb.py, 1500 flows each):
  side A (out tb470 eth1): src 02:00:0a:00:00:01 .. 02:00:0a:00:05:dc
  side B (out tb470 eth2): src 02:00:0b:00:00:01 .. 02:00:0b:00:05:dc
A[i] addresses B[i] and vice versa, so after one pass in each direction the DUT
holds 3000 entries and every frame is known-unicast (no flooding).
Frames are Ether/IP/UDP:5000, 96 bytes.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STEP 3 — Start the traffic. Expected: traffic should forward               PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
tcpreplay --pps=3000 --loop=3 on each side => 4500 frames sent per side.
Counted by SOURCE MAC with tshark (see CAVEAT 1):

  capture on eth1 : side-A src 4500 frames / 1500 MACs   (its own TX)
                    side-B src 4500 frames / 1500 MACs   <- received through DUT
  capture on eth2 : side-B src 4500 frames / 1500 MACs   (its own TX)
                    side-A src 4500 frames / 1500 MACs   <- received through DUT

4500 sent = 4500 received in each direction. ZERO LOSS, all 1500 distinct
source MACs present at the far end in both directions.

Continuous traffic then started for steps 4-7:
  tcpreplay -i eth1 --pps=1000 --loop=0 fdb-a.pcap
  tcpreplay -i eth2 --pps=1000 --loop=0 fdb-b.pcap

CAVEAT 1 — counting frames with `tcpdump | grep <mac-prefix>` DOUBLES the count,
because the far side's frames carry those same MACs as DESTINATIONS. The A-side
MACs appear in eth2's capture both as sources (received) and as destinations of
eth2's own transmissions. Must count on the source field only
(`tshark -e eth.src`). First pass here read 9000 instead of 4500 for this reason.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STEP 4 — Check the FDB table. Expected: same across all stack members     PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The test case says `show platform table fdb`. That command DOES NOT EXIST on
IE520 — `show platform table ?` offers:
  internalports, ip, ipmulti, ipmulti-count, ipv6, ipv6multi, macaddr,
  macvxlan, mclinklist, mcv6linklist, rule-counters
The IE520 equivalent is `show platform table macaddr`.
Also note `remote-diff all show platform table macaddr` is REJECTED
(remote-diff does not accept `platform`); `show platform table macaddr` already
prints BOTH members' tables in one output, so remote-diff is not needed for it.

(a) Software MAC table:
      awplus# remote-diff all show mac address-table
      % Comparing "show mac address-table" on node 1 and node 2
      % Results are identical

(b) Hardware/ASIC table — `show platform table macaddr`:
      Stack member 1: [Instance 0]  Total number of entries = 3003
      Stack member 2: [Instance 1]  Total number of entries = 3003

    Programmatic comparison of all 3001 parsed data rows:
      same MAC set ......................................... True
      same Index/VID/OutPort/Trunk/Perm/DA_r/Skip for EVERY MAC ... True

    Two columns legitimately differ and are NOT a sync failure:
      Dev   — member 1 reports 0, member 2 reports 1. This is the local ASIC
              instance index (member 1 is "[Instance 0]", member 2 is
              "[Instance 1]"). It must differ; it is each unit's own identity.
      Aging — a transient per-ASIC refresh bit. The two members' tables are
              dumped sequentially, seconds apart, with traffic at 1000 pps, so
              this samples different instants. Distribution at capture:
                member1: po1 1501/po2 1493 with Aging=1, 7 with Aging=0
                member2: po1 1498/po2  320 with Aging=1, 1183 with Aging=0

    Sample of the same index on both members, showing everything else matching:
      m1:  0   0200.0b00.0151  393  po2  0  1  1  0  -  dyn 0
      m2:  0   0200.0b00.0151  393  po2  0  0  1  1  -  dyn 0
                                          ^        ^
                                        Aging     Dev

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STEP 5 — Reboot the stack master. Expected: recover within 6 seconds      PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Master at the time was member 1. Command: `reboot stack-member 1` (confirmed
"reboot stack master? (y/n): y"), issued at epoch 1787089053.395 with
continuous traffic running and tcpdump capturing both ends.

Outage measured as the largest inter-arrival gap in frames RECEIVED through
the DUT (source-MAC filtered):

  A->B  (eth1 -> AR4050 -> DUT -> x230 -> eth2)
        318060 frames over a 318.1 s window
        largest gap  0.022 s  starting +2.063 s after the reboot command
        next         0.016 s          +3.005 s
        next         0.003 s        +182.341 s

  B->A  (eth2 -> x230 -> DUT -> AR4050 -> eth1)
        337386 frames over a 338.2 s window
        largest gap  0.814 s  starting +2.060 s after the reboot command
        next         0.003 s        +305.345 s
        next         0.002 s        +145.350 s

WORST-CASE OUTAGE 814 ms vs a 6000 ms expectation — PASS with ~7x margin.
Both directions break at the same instant (+2.06 s), which is when member 1's
ports actually dropped. Everything after that is normal jitter (<25 ms).

Traffic continued for the entire capture window even though member 1 did NOT
come back during it (see FINDING below) — the surviving LAG leg on member 2
carried it. That is the resiliency the test is really measuring.

