================================================================================
FINDING (not part of the test case, but it dominated the run)
BOOTLOADER NETBOOT OVERRIDE - 'boot system' in AW+ DOES NOT CONTROL IT
================================================================================
Terrence asked for a fresh .rel in flash with default boot set to flash. Doing
that at the AW+ level was NOT sufficient, and the distinction cost real time.

Done first, and verified:
  copy tftp://10.38.215.33/IE520-tb470.rel flash:/IE520-tb470.rel  (41,103,847 B)
  configure terminal ; boot system flash:/IE520-tb470.rel ; write
  -> "Synchronizing file across the stack, please wait..."
     "File synchronization with stack member 2 successfully completed"
  -> show boot: Current boot image : flash:/IE520-tb470.rel (file exists)
  -> flash free: member 1 101.5M -> 63.4M , member 2 64.5M -> 26.3M
     (both members hold the release; the stack auto-sync did member 2)

Despite all of that, rebooting member 1 still NETBOOTED, and failed:
  Warning: System has been forced to boot from a non-standard location
  To restore the system to default behaviour, enter the Boot Menu,
      Choose option 2, then option 9
  Loading tftp://10.38.215.33/IE520-tb470.rel via 'eth0' interface...
  Waiting for Ethernet connection... unable to connect.
  TFTP from server 10.38.215.33; our IP address is 10.38.215.39
  ARP Retry count exceeded; starting again
  ERROR: Failed to load tftp://10.38.215.33/IE520-tb470.rel
  Restarting system in ...  543210
=> BOOT LOOP. (The same loop Terrence had hit earlier in the day on this unit.)
The netboot runs over the ASIX USB-Ethernet dongle, which could not reach
10.38.215.33 - unsurprising, since the DUT's front-panel ports had been moved
into vlan 393 / the LAGs for this test.

FIX - at the BOOTLOADER, via Ctrl+B at boot:
  Boot Menu -> 2. Change the default boot source (for advanced users)
            -> 9. Boot from default (determined by main CLI)
  "Saving settings... Saving Environment to SPIFlash... done / OK / Complete"

That submenu states the separation explicitly:
  "NOTE: These settings are specific to the Bootloader.
   They are not related in any way to what may be configured
   by the 'boot system' command in the main software release."

Verified on the very next boot:
  Loading flash:IE520-tb470.rel...
  ## Loading kernel from FIT Image at 04000000 ...
     Verifying Hash Integrity ... sha1+ OK
No TFTP, no loop, and the unit rejoined the stack normally.

LESSON: on IE520, 'show boot' reporting "Current boot image: flash:/x.rel"
proves NOTHING about where the unit will actually boot from. The bootloader
override is invisible from the AW+ CLI and only announces itself in the boot
banner. Check the banner, or check Boot Menu option 2.

APPLIED TO BOTH MEMBERS - 2026-08-19, after the test run.
Member 2 was rebooted ('reboot stack-member 2', issued from member 1's console
in privileged mode - note 'reboot' is rejected at user-exec) with a Ctrl+B
catcher already listening on /dev/u4, and the same Boot Menu 2 -> 9 fix applied:
  Saving settings... Complete
  Loading flash:IE520-tb470.rel...
Member 2 rejoined and reached Ready. Final state:
  ID  MAC              Priority  Status  Role
  1   84e3.2787.0740   128       Ready   Active Master
  2   84e3.2787.09c0   128       Ready   Backup Member
  Operational Status  Normal operation
  Current boot image  flash:/IE520-tb470.rel (file exists)
  flash free          member 1 63.4M , member 2 26.3M  (both hold the release)
Both LAGs re-formed automatically after the reboot with all four legs
synchronized, and all four ports returned to vlan 393 at a-full/a-1000.

Member 2 sat in "Syncing" noticeably longer than member 1 did (several minutes
vs under one), while 'show stack detail' read "Normal operation" and both stack
ports showed "Learnt neighbor" throughout. It reached Ready on its own, and the
LAGs and data path were fully restored while it was still Syncing - so Syncing
is not a traffic-affecting state.

Also visible in 'show stack detail' and UNCHANGED by any of this: the resiliency
link still reads "Configured" on the master and "Failed" on the backup - the
open defect characterised in test 17688. Not exercised by this test.

================================================================================
CAVEATS / THINGS THAT WOULD MISLEAD A RE-RUN
================================================================================
1. Counting frames with 'tcpdump | grep <mac-prefix>' DOUBLE-COUNTS, because the
   far side's frames carry those same MACs as DESTINATIONS. Count on the source
   field only ('tshark -e eth.src'). First pass here read 9000 instead of 4500.
2. 'show platform table fdb' does not exist on IE520; the equivalent is
   'show platform table macaddr'. 'remote-diff' does not accept 'platform', but
   'show platform table macaddr' already prints BOTH members' tables in one go.
3. The 'Dev' column MUST differ between members (it is the local ASIC instance
   index: member 1 is "[Instance 0]", member 2 is "[Instance 1]") and the 'Aging'
   bit is a transient sampled at different instants. Neither is a sync failure.
   Compare Index/VID/OutPort/Trunk/Perm/DA_r/Skip.
4. 'lacp global-passive-mode enable' silently auto-creates single-member
   aggregators the moment a neighbour starts LACP, then blocks edits to those
   ports ("% Cannot configure aggregator member." /
   "% ... cannot be aggregated due to mismatching link configuration").
   Turn it off, clear the dynamic groups, normalise the ports, then build LAGs.
5. The DUT's TFTP copy of a 41 MB release takes ~60 s and prints NOTHING between
   "Copying..." and completion. A driver that gives up on silence will look like
   a hang when the transfer is in fact succeeding. Wait for the PROMPT, not for
   output. This produced two false "the copy is hung" conclusions here; the file
   had actually landed at the correct byte count both times.
6. Two processes on one serial port silently eat each other's bytes. An orphaned
   console driver left behind by a killed ssh reads as "the switch is
   unresponsive". Check 'fuser -v /dev/ttyUSBn' before concluding anything about
   a console. And never 'pkill -f <script>' over ssh - the pattern matches the
   ssh command line carrying it and kills your own session (exit 255). Kill by
   PID from 'pgrep'.
7. Port-to-port mapping on this bench must be MEASURED (shut a neighbour port,
   see which DUT port drops). MAC-table inference goes stale the moment anyone
   recables, and a verbal description of a cable run is easy to read backwards.

